# Sizing & single-mechanism (HARD requirement — verification-easy regime)

The loop only CONVERGES on tickets a worker can land in ONE small PR. Large,
multi-mechanism tickets do not converge: the worker can't land the diff, the
critic correctly blocks it, and the slot is wasted. So every *ticket* you file
(items in `proposed_tickets`) MUST be sized for the verification-easy regime:

  * **ONE mechanism.** A single, named change — one code path, one feature
    flag, one command, one endpoint, one guard. If you find yourself writing
    "and" between two distinct behaviours in the title, it is two tickets.
  * **ONE primary falsifiable acceptance criterion.** State it as a single
    sentence a test can prove true/false. Secondary criteria are fine only if
    they are facets of that one mechanism (not separate features).
  * **≤ ~150 net LOC.** Estimate implementation + tests at no more than ~150
    net lines of code (this mirrors the worker's `scope_soft_loc_cap = 150`).
    If your honest estimate exceeds that, the idea is an EPIC — decompose it
    (see below). Do NOT file an oversized idea as a single ticket.
  * **Implementable + testable in one small PR** by a single worker session.

When in doubt, file the SMALLER thing. A narrow ticket that lands beats a
broad ticket that never converges.

# Decomposing large / multi-mechanism ideas into an epic + sub-tickets

If an idea is inherently large or spans more than one mechanism (e.g. "auto-
sweep stale branches" = sweep-on-merge AND a periodic sweep command AND a
dry-run report), DO NOT file it as one ticket. Instead file it as:

  1. ONE entry in `proposed_epics` — the umbrella idea. It carries the axis +
     customer_story but is NOT itself dispatchable (it is filed with the
     `epic` label, never `loop:ready`).
  2. 2–4 entries in `proposed_tickets`, EACH a single-mechanism, ≤ ~150-LOC,
     verification-easy slice of that epic. Each sub-ticket's body MUST name
     the parent epic by its title (write `Part of epic: "<epic title>"` on the
     first line of the body) and stand on its own as one shippable PR.

Example decomposition — "auto-sweep stale branches":
  * epic:    "Auto-sweep stale branches"
  * ticket:  "Delete the source branch when its PR merges" (one mechanism)
  * ticket:  "Add `forge-loop sweep-branches` periodic command" (one mechanism)
  * ticket:  "Dry-run flag for sweep-branches that lists, never deletes" (one)

Only the small sub-tickets are dispatchable; the epic groups them. Never file
an epic WITHOUT at least 2 sub-tickets — a bare epic moves nothing.

# Hard-refusal rubric

A proposal is REJECTED if any of the following are true:

1. It omits the `axis` field, or `axis` is empty.
2. It omits the `customer_story` field, or `customer_story` is empty.
3. Its `axis` value does not match a `name` from the axes block above
   (exact, case-sensitive).
4. Its title or body contains (case-insensitive substring) any phrase
   listed under that axis's `rejected_as_cosmetic`.
5. A `proposed_tickets` item is multi-mechanism or estimated to exceed
   ~150 net LOC — it must be decomposed into an epic + sub-tickets instead.

These are not stylistic preferences — proposals failing the rubric are
dropped post-hoc by the operator. Do not propose:
  * rename / refactor work whose only value is tidiness
  * comment / docstring polishing that doesn't change behavior
  * reflows, formatter-equivalent diffs, or "cleanup" without a customer

DO propose work that:
  * cites a specific axis by `name`
  * carries a `customer_story` — a verbatim quote or paraphrase tied to
    that axis's `customer` field — explaining *who* benefits and *how*
  * is not already represented by an open epic or open ticket above

